## Why the RED is legitimate, and what the one green test is

`npx vitest run --project worker test/task-list-filters.test.ts` before any
production change: **19 failed, 1 passed**.

The 19 failures are all the same root cause, which is the correct one: Hono
ignores query parameters no handler reads, so `?from=…`, `?to=…` and
`?priority=…` are dropped and the route answers `200` with the **whole** list.
Every membership assertion therefore fails on content (extra rows), and every
`400` assertion fails on status (`200` received). No failure is a crash, a
missing import, or a reference to a symbol that does not exist — the suite
exercises the shipped route through its real HTTP surface and finds the
behaviour absent, which is what a test-first RED is supposed to look like.

The single passing test — *"still rejects an invalid limit when filters are
present"* — is deliberate and is **not** a trivial assert. `limit` validation
shipped in unit 2; this case pins that adding three `WHERE` clauses does not
disturb it. It is a composition regression guard, green before and required to
stay green after, and it is the only test here whose subject already exists.

**One defect this suite caught in itself before review.** The first draft of
the AC-6 composition fixture ordered its rows so that the two matching Tasks
were also the two globally most urgent — which made *"takes the first N of the
ORDERED FILTERED set"* pass with no filtering at all: a test that could not
fail. The fixture was rearranged so the two rows an unfiltered request returns
first (`N1 normal oldest`, wrong priority; `H5 done high`, wrong status) are
precisely the two the filters remove. The test now fails pre-implementation and
discriminates a limit-before-filter implementation from a correct one.

